00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.310
PROFESSOR: So a very
brief recap of where

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.470
we were in the last lecture.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:38.290
We talked about electronic
band structure in crystals.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:43.240
The lecture before we
said 1D, and we briefly

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.320
extended to 3D, now extension
just show you how 3D look like.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.100
And then we start talking
about real crystals, a Bravais

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.930
lattice and crystal bonding,
how the crystal different

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.230
type of bonds,
when they is bond,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.130
ionic bonding, covalent
bonding, metallic bonding.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:12.880
And then we talk, at the
end we say reciprocal space,

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.300
how we represent
periodic 3D functions

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.130
and using the Fourier transform.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:27.280
And for that, we need to
say the inverse of this

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.270
in 3D Fourier image is
in the reciprocal space.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.890
So if we have a real
lattice, real space

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.910
lattice with a primitive
lattice vectors, a1, a2, a3,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.260
we say we can--

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.680
the corresponding
reciprocal lattice vector

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.080
is a b1, b2, b3, where
b here, b1 is a2, a3.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.280
And b2 you will just
permute the index.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.000
So b2 is a3 cross a1.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.930
So with that, you
can construct--

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.810
this is the primitive
lattice vector.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.460
You can construct
array of points

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.820
using the reciprocal
lattice vector G.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.940
And this is, let's
say, this is the way

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.200
you construct a
reciprocal space.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.060
And correspondingly, you
have a primitive unit cell

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.760
in reciprocal space.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.470
And also regional sites
cell, that's a unique way

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.100
construct a unit cell,
primitive unit cell.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.950
And regular size
cell, there is also

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.250
not a le-- is this
smallest volume

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.160
is the first Brillouin zone.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:50.650
And now, if you take a real
space last vector T, which

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.460
consists of an integer n1,
n2, n3, combination of a1,

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:59.350
a2, a3, and you take the
reciprocal lattice vector G.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.300
And then you do the dot product.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.670
And then you say, because
of the characteristics,

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.970
this is a volume
for the real space.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.465
So because the characteristics
of this cross product,

00:03:10.465 --> 00:03:17.810
you find out that G dot T is
just 2 pi times the integer.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:22.070
So those are purely
the reciprocal space.

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:25.070
I was joking, it's really
mathematical concept.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:29.590
It's a Fourier
transform of real space.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:35.050
And so, I say, it turns
out, it's much easier,

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:39.570
like when we-- electrical
engineer always use frequency.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.120
And physicists like
to represent areas

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.310
and in the reciprocal space.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:50.440
And also, at the
end of last lecture,

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.030
I talked about the
Bragg condition.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:58.230
I give you, if you have
a periodic structure,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.970
you have a wave comes in.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:03.900
And I have a
crystal, for example.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.430
And the wave comes to.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.450
It could be X-ray, it
could be an electron wave,

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:13.765
in transmission, electron
microscopy is electron wave,

00:04:13.765 --> 00:04:16.320
and X-ray you have photon waves.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.670
And you use a detector.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.570
So a wave has a wave
vector k incident.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:26.880
And let's say, this is the
final outgoing wave vector.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.670
And it turns out, if you have
a periodic structure here,

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:36.960
crystal, and the condition
where you can detect--

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.430
this is you detector.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:39.720
This is your source.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.200
The condition you
can detect a signal

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:52.980
is when ki minus kf plus
or minus G equals 0.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.760
So this is the Bragg condition.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:04.860
And then we give this in--
by the end of last lecture,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:10.440
and we went to Google image and
