01:01:33.270 --> 01:01:35.640
PROFESSOR: My original
harmonic oscillator--

01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:37.810
so a very good question.

01:01:37.810 --> 01:01:42.210
I remember, for example,
I just mentioned glass.

01:01:42.210 --> 01:01:43.020
It turns out that--

01:01:43.020 --> 01:01:46.590
I also mentioned
the hydrogen atom.

01:01:46.590 --> 01:01:52.170
And turns out that in physics,
when people talk of phonon,

01:01:52.170 --> 01:01:55.050
they talk about
the extended modes.

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:59.490
So hydrogen atom
vibration, people

01:01:59.490 --> 01:02:03.000
mostly do not call it a phonon.

01:02:03.000 --> 01:02:07.590
And once I went to
a phonon conference.

01:02:07.590 --> 01:02:12.390
And I ask, say, a very
famous guy, I say,

01:02:12.390 --> 01:02:15.630
what about the phonons in glass?

01:02:15.630 --> 01:02:19.050
Because the glass is not a
periodic, it's not a crystal.

01:02:19.050 --> 01:02:22.500
And oh, nobody knows, he said.

01:02:22.500 --> 01:02:24.840
But still have those modes.

01:02:24.840 --> 01:02:35.340
And but see, you ask, there
is an even more profound, say,

01:02:35.340 --> 01:02:37.170
question one could
ask in this direction.

01:02:37.170 --> 01:02:40.800
Because when you
go to a third term,

01:02:40.800 --> 01:02:44.190
here a potential is a
harmonic, second term.

01:02:44.190 --> 01:02:46.020
Go to a third term,
that's the scattering.

01:02:46.020 --> 01:02:48.120
That's the two phonons
start to couple.

01:02:48.120 --> 01:02:52.470
And this is, in fact,
very different for light.

01:02:52.470 --> 01:02:56.880
Light normally hard to
couple to each other.

01:02:56.880 --> 01:03:00.300
But phonon, you easily
couple to each other

01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:02.530
because of this third term.

01:03:02.530 --> 01:03:06.180
But if that third
term is so strong,

01:03:06.180 --> 01:03:11.400
and it can come back to
affect your dispersion.

01:03:11.400 --> 01:03:15.090
And there's-- I do not
know good ways to do it.

01:03:15.090 --> 01:03:17.460
But there are people
publishing paper, for example,

01:03:17.460 --> 01:03:19.860
recently there was a
paper on the soft phonon

01:03:19.860 --> 01:03:24.150
in [? Telluride. ?] And because
of the strong enharmonic

01:03:24.150 --> 01:03:28.020
interaction, that changed a
lot on the phonon dispersion.

01:03:30.780 --> 01:03:34.650
Then the question is, if the
phonon scattering is so strong,

01:03:34.650 --> 01:03:35.940
you break the wave.

01:03:35.940 --> 01:03:38.580
I say, this basic picture
is I have extended waves

01:03:38.580 --> 01:03:40.680
through the whole crystal.

01:03:40.680 --> 01:03:42.810
But say if it
breaks the wave, it

01:03:42.810 --> 01:03:45.930
doesn't say if the
universe of this frequency

01:03:45.930 --> 01:03:50.480
is to give you [INAUDIBLE] time
for one period of vibration.

01:03:50.480 --> 01:03:54.320
But if they-- you went
to break this, say,

01:03:54.320 --> 01:03:57.500
wave is even faster
than the one period,

01:03:57.500 --> 01:04:00.920
then I don't see how people--

01:04:00.920 --> 01:04:02.960
I do not know how people
are describing those.

01:04:08.520 --> 01:04:09.570
Let's see.

01:04:09.570 --> 01:04:13.920
The next is the approximation.

01:04:19.890 --> 01:04:26.220
If you recall for electrons,
we say at the bottom,

01:04:26.220 --> 01:04:27.750
we use a parabolic.

01:04:27.750 --> 01:04:32.160
We say at the bottom in
this region, the first order

01:04:32.160 --> 01:04:37.290
dispersion, the first
order derivative is 0.

01:04:37.290 --> 01:04:42.430
And so, I use a parabola
to represent electrons.

01:04:42.430 --> 01:04:48.240
And for phonons, for optical
phonon and acoustic phonon,

01:04:48.240 --> 01:04:49.950
you look at it.

01:04:49.950 --> 01:04:52.650
It's like a sine function.

01:04:52.650 --> 01:04:55.650
And then, the
simplest approximation

01:04:55.650 --> 01:05:02.930
that people have made, one is
a linear function rather than

01:05:02.930 --> 01:05:05.190
the sine function.

01:05:05.190 --> 01:05:08.880
And this is a--
the order is flat.

01:05:08.880 --> 01:05:12.680
That's more appropriate
for optical phonon,

01:05:12.680 --> 01:05:15.750
and this is more appropriate
for acoustic phonon.

01:05:15.750 --> 01:05:21.413
And this approximation
is Debye approximation.

01:05:21.413 --> 01:05:22.455
And this is the Einstein.

01:05:26.840 --> 01:05:30.320
It turns out that Einstein
first created this model for

01:05:30.320 --> 01:05:35.110
to model the specific
heat of a crystal.

01:05:35.110 --> 01:05:37.210
But say, it's not valid.

01:05:37.210 --> 01:05:39.000
See, it doesn't agree
with the experiment

01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:41.440
when we go to very
low temperature.

01:05:41.440 --> 01:05:43.520
That's because when you
go very low temperature,

01:05:43.520 --> 01:05:46.680
most phonons are here.

01:05:46.680 --> 01:05:50.920
And so, the specific
measurement doesn't agree.

01:05:50.920 --> 01:05:55.960
So Debye introduced
the linear dispersion.

01:05:55.960 --> 01:05:59.170
So those are two
different dispersion.

01:05:59.170 --> 01:06:02.710
And the corresponding frequency,
this is the Einstein frequency,

01:06:02.710 --> 01:06:07.310
and this is the Debye frequency.

01:06:07.310 --> 01:06:10.550
And in a real
crystal, Debye model,

01:06:10.550 --> 01:06:12.530
very often we will do the--

01:06:12.530 --> 01:06:14.870
later on when we're talking
specific heat-- people

01:06:14.870 --> 01:06:17.240
typically use Debye model.

01:06:17.240 --> 01:06:24.440
And this k, of course,
I only draw 1D.

01:06:24.440 --> 01:06:28.310
But in real crystal is different
direction, have different k.

01:06:28.310 --> 01:06:32.770
But Debye model actually
is a isotropic crystal.

01:06:32.770 --> 01:06:36.550
So it approximate not only
linear in one direction,

01:06:36.550 --> 01:06:41.480
just to use one direction
represent the whole crystal.

01:06:41.480 --> 01:06:45.760
So that's-- with that, there are
some relations in terms of what

01:06:45.760 --> 01:06:50.470
you convert, what's the
corresponding Debye lattice

01:06:50.470 --> 01:06:52.666
constant here.

01:06:52.666 --> 01:06:56.430
You have to count the total
modes to be consistent.

01:07:00.270 --> 01:07:08.580
So where we are going from
here is in the next lecture,

01:07:08.580 --> 01:07:11.460
I'll continue to come back
to, say, use this simplest

01:07:11.460 --> 01:07:14.250
approximation, Debye
model, Einstein

01:07:14.250 --> 01:07:18.590
model, to count degeneracy.

01:07:18.590 --> 01:07:21.860
Before, when we
talk about the, say,

01:07:21.860 --> 01:07:26.030
hydrogen atom, electrons, we
say degeneracy is 2n square.

01:07:26.030 --> 01:07:30.390
That's how you understand
periodic table.

01:07:30.390 --> 01:07:32.970
But now, if you
look at this, if I

01:07:32.970 --> 01:07:39.005
draw in one crystallographic
direction, this is pi over a.

01:07:39.005 --> 01:07:43.800
And in another direction there
could be another dispersion.

01:07:43.800 --> 01:07:46.560
So this represents
a different mode.

01:07:46.560 --> 01:07:48.960
But they could have
the same frequency.

01:07:51.630 --> 01:07:55.950
So that same frequency
means the same energy.

01:07:55.950 --> 01:07:59.160
And I can have many modes
that have the same energy.

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:01.950
And I have to count
that degeneracy.

01:08:01.950 --> 01:08:04.320
And that counting
is very important

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:08.000
if you want to do
any calculation.

01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:09.470
It's a mathematical concept.

01:08:09.470 --> 01:08:11.130
That's a density of states.

01:08:11.130 --> 01:08:15.110
We're going from degeneracy
to density of states.

01:08:15.110 --> 01:08:17.189
And that we'll do
in the next lecture.

