01:16:37.290 --> 01:16:41.980
And a quantum cascade laser is--

01:16:41.980 --> 01:16:44.950
a typically laser is you
have a conduction band,

01:16:44.950 --> 01:16:47.470
you have a valence band.

01:16:47.470 --> 01:16:48.830
And you put the electrons.

01:16:48.830 --> 01:16:50.650
So you actually put a current--

01:16:50.650 --> 01:16:53.230
semiconductor, you put
a current into here.

01:16:53.230 --> 01:16:57.310
You put a lot of electrons
from an external injection.

01:16:57.310 --> 01:17:02.560
You put a lot of holes to here.

01:17:02.560 --> 01:17:05.620
And now, electron will
fall from here to here

01:17:05.620 --> 01:17:06.910
and give off a photon.

01:17:06.910 --> 01:17:10.330
That's the emission process.

01:17:10.330 --> 01:17:12.610
Of course, laser is
stimulating emission.

01:17:12.610 --> 01:17:16.240
And this is a typical
semiconductor laser.

01:17:16.240 --> 01:17:20.890
A quantum cascade laser is
you use that-- let's say,

01:17:20.890 --> 01:17:24.010
this is a conduction band.

01:17:24.010 --> 01:17:29.350
And typically, we just
look at gallium arsenide.

01:17:29.350 --> 01:17:33.270
But when you make that
gallium arsenide very thin,

01:17:33.270 --> 01:17:34.260
we have a quantum well.

01:17:34.260 --> 01:17:35.950
We talk about the quantum well.

01:17:35.950 --> 01:17:40.080
So this is in 3D.

01:17:40.080 --> 01:17:42.900
Now, when you are one direction,
you make it very thin.

01:17:42.900 --> 01:17:48.240
So the energies start to
discretize in that direction.

01:17:48.240 --> 01:17:54.680
So my wave vector space then
becomes very large, basically.

01:17:54.680 --> 01:18:00.380
And so, it doesn't use the
electron to hold recombination

